<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/advanced/economic-and-political-anthropology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Economic and political anthropology asks <strong>what exchange and power look like outside
    the market-and-state framework</strong> — and in doing so, shows both to be historical
    designs rather than human defaults. The comparative record is the discipline's
    counter-laboratory to economics and political science.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the gift, embeddedness, object biographies, order without states" widget="equationStep" config={econanthCfg} />

  <h2>Findings that reframe the "given"</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reciprocity has three modes:</strong> generalised (give without accounting — kin), balanced (even trade), negative (get the better — markets at their purest). Sahlins mapped them onto social distance: how you exchange encodes who someone is to you.</li>
    <li><strong>The kula ring:</strong> Malinowski's Trobriand finding — dangerous voyages to exchange shell necklaces and armbands for prestige, with practical trade riding along. Circulation for relationship, not consumption.</li>
    <li><strong>Scott's "Seeing Like a State" (1998):</strong> high-modernist states simplify society to legibility (surnames, cadastral maps, monoculture) — making populations governable and fragile. The anthropologist's view of why grand plans fail.</li>
    <li><strong>Behavioural economics converges:</strong> the economics lesson's "predictably irrational" humans look like Mauss's obligated reciprocators — two traditions, one finding, from opposite directions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mauss, The Gift (1925); Polanyi, The Great Transformation (1944); Sahlins, Stone Age Economics (1972); Scott, Weapons of the Weak (1985) and Seeing Like a State (1998).</p>
</TopicPage>
